  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1158 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nClearing skies this afternoon with temperatures already in the\nmid-to-upper 80s as of 11AM. Satellite shows broad ridging in\nthe upper-level flow centered over western Kansas and the Texas\nPanhandle today, with a series of ridge-riding waves continuing\nto produce lingering showers over Kansas and Missouri. With the\nstorm track set up farther south today, storm chances tonight \nare low, with only a 20-30 chance storms may clip our far \nsoutheast Nebraska counties. \n\nWednesday we'll be watching a trough move into the PacNW,\namplifying ridging over the Missouri River Valley. This likely \nwill lead to temperatures reaching a couple degrees warmer than\ntoday. With dew points staying in the mid 60s to low 70s, heat \nindices shouldn't get back up above 100. \n\nWednesday night into Thursday as the ridge shifts off to the\neast we'll see return flow from the south start to advect more\nmoisture back into the region. At the same time a series of \nvorticity maxima will move through as the trough travels overtop\nthe crest of the ridge in the upper-level flow. This will bring\na few rounds of showers and storms starting overnight Wednesday\nthrough Thursday night. The most substantial round of storms \nwill be Thursday night when the vort max moves through and is \naided by enhanced shear along the nose of the nocturnal low- \nlevel jet. This could potentially lead to severe storms and \nheavy rainfall for somewhere across eastern Nebraska, western \nIowa, eastern South Dakota or southwest Minnesota. This is also \nwhen storm chances from the NBM increase to 70 to 90 percent, \nindicating great agreement in our suite of models in showers and\nstorms through this time period.\n\nOn the back-side of this system on Friday we see a fairly strong\npush of northerly flow bringing in cooler air out of Canada.\nTemperatures on Friday are only forecast to max out in the low-\nto-mid 80s. Smoke may be a greater concern on Friday right\nbehind the front as subsidence likely will pull some of the\nwildfire smoke aloft down toward the surface. \n\nMild, dry weather continues into Saturday with temperatures\nstarting to warm back up on Sunday into early next week. Clearly\nwe just can't keep nice temperatures for long this time of year\nas another ridge builds into the Central Plains.",
